{
  "case": "latest signup date",
  "variant": "with",
  "trial": 0,
  "run": {
    "final_text": "I wrote `latest.py` and ran it. It prints only the most recent signup date, as the task required:\n\n```\n2025-10-12\n```\n\nThat's Priya's signup, the latest of the five users.\n\n**The guide is out of date.** The `acme-client-guide` skill says `created_at` is in milliseconds and should be divided by 1000. The installed `acme_client.py` is version 2.0, and its docstring says `created_at` is now in **seconds** (1.x used milliseconds). The values confirm this: 1760227200 as seconds is 2025-10-12. So the script uses the timestamp as-is. Following the guide would have put every date in January 1970.\n\nThe guide is also wrong about active users. In 2.0 you should check `user[\"active\"]`. The `state` field is kept only for backwards compatibility and is always `\"enabled\"`, so counting with it, as the guide suggests, would mark every user as active. This script doesn't use either field, but the guide should be updated before other code relies on it.",
